            0.816,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Measuring Space: Perimeter and Area"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "125"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.108,
            0.732,
            0.13
          ],
          "content": "India) is celebrated each year as **Pi Approximation Day**."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.153,
            0.587,
            0.176
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.4 Length of an Arc of a Circle"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.195,
            0.421,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.156,
                0.35,
                0.442,
                0.384
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.8: Two semicircles making a full circle"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.483,
            0.201,
            0.877,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "The circumference of a circle of diameter d is π d. Since the diameter is twice the radius, we can also write the circumference of a circle as 2πr, where r is the radius."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.483,
            0.297,
            0.878,
            0.355
          ],
          "content": "What will be the length of a **semicircle** with the same radius r (see Fig. 6.8)?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.483,
            0.354,
            0.877,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": "If we reflect the circle in the diameter AB, the red semicircle and the blue semicircle exchange places. This means that they have equal length, which is $2\\pi r \\div 2 = \\pi r$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.464,
            0.886,
            0.543
          ],
          "content": "Instead of reflecting the figure in the diameter AB, we can also imagine rotating the whole figure around the centre O through an angle of $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ }$ , i.e., through a half-turn. The effect is the same, and we get the same formula for the length of the semicircular arc."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.554,
            0.449,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.175,
                0.74,
                0.424,
                0.773
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.9: Four quarter circles make a full circle"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.484,
            0.573,
            0.877,
            0.613
          ],
          "content": "What will be the length of a **quarter circle** with the same radius (Fig. 6.9)?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.483,
            0.613,
            0.877,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": "Imagine rotating the entire figure through $9 0 ^ { \\circ } ,$ , clockwise or anticlockwise. Each quarter circle moves and covers another quarter circle. So each quarter circle has the same arc length, and this is equal to $2 \\pi r \\div 4 = \\frac{\\pi r}{2}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.781,
            0.327,
            0.801
          ],
          "content": "Note the following:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.808,
            0.885,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": "• The formula for the length of a semicircle may also be written as $2 \\pi r \\times \\frac { 1 8 0 ^ { \\circ } } { 3 6 0 ^ { \\circ } }$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
